# Output can lag behind actual progress -- give a job a full hour before assuming it's hung

User-confirmed cluster quirk (07-22): this system's job output stream
sometimes lags behind actual progress for no discernible reason -- the
`.err`/`.out` files and `metrics.jsonl` can appear static even though the
job is running fine underneath. **Rule of thumb: if it's been less than an
hour since the job started (or since its last visible progress), treat it as
still running with a delayed output stream, not hung.** Only consider
cancelling after a full hour with zero progress.

On top of that general quirk, there is also a specific, well-understood
reason a FRESH job looks stalled right after its first step (see below) --
but even that is really just a special case of "give it more time before
judging," not a separate exception to the one-hour rule.

# A fresh job's first logged step is normally silent for ~20 minutes

`main.py`'s training loop runs a full validation pass unconditionally at
step 1 (`if step == 1 or step % args.eval_every == 0`), not just at the
configured `eval_every` cadence. With ~1013 validation examples at
`batch_size=1`, this takes roughly **1195 seconds (~20 minutes)** -- confirmed
from `UU`'s own historical step-1 record (`val_components.duration_sec`).

During this window, the job's `.err`/`.out` files stop growing and
`metrics.jsonl` stays empty (the tqdm postfix for step 1 shows loss/acc, but
the actual `metrics_file.write()` only happens AFTER validation completes,
later in the code path). This looks exactly like a hang if you only check
elapsed time and file mtimes, but it is completely normal.

## Before concluding a fresh job is hung

1. Check how long it's been since the job started or since its last visible
   progress. **Under one hour: assume it's fine, just laggy output or mid
   first-validation-pass.** Do not cancel.
2. Only escalate to "probably hung" after a full hour with zero progress
   (no new `metrics.jsonl` entries, `.err`/`.out` completely static).
3. Do not cancel and resubmit reflexively -- this wastes GPU time restarting
   a job that was working fine, and (if it happens to land on a busy node
   again) can look like a recurring "hang" that is actually the same normal
   behavior repeating. This mistake was made twice in a row on 07-22 before
   the real cause was found (see `TRAINING_RUN_LOG.md`).

## The same trap applies to SMOKE TESTS, not just monitoring a real job

07-27: burned well over an hour "debugging a hang" in a brand-new
`main.py` feature (the in-training kitchen-sink probe, `--probe_every`)
that turned out to have no bug at all -- the smoke test used a short
`srun --time` budget with `--val_fraction` left non-zero, so step 1's
unconditional full validation pass silently ate the entire budget before
the new feature's own code ever ran. Every symptom (no new output, 100%
GPU utilization, killed by time limit right as step 1's tqdm postfix
finally flushed) matched this exact rule -- it should have been the first
hypothesis checked, not the last.

**When writing a quick smoke test for a NEW feature/change:** either pass
`--val_fraction 0` (skip validation entirely -- the fastest way to get a
clean, fast smoke run) or size the `srun --time` budget for the ~20 minute
step-1 validation pass if validation itself needs exercising. Don't spend
time hypothesizing about DataLoader workers, tokenizer fork deadlocks,
gradient checkpointing, LoRA, or cluster contention before ruling this out
first -- it is by far the most common cause of a "hung" first step.
